{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming 4f38749f. I read the code before claiming and the defect is mine, from the configuration custody merge d4f54392.\n\nabandonPartialConfig at internal/apphost/config.go:201 decides what to delete with os.SameFile(created, current). On Unix that compares device and inode. An inode number is not a durable identity: unlink the created file, let something else create a replacement at the same path, and the kernel may hand that replacement the same inode. SameFile then answers true and the cleanup deletes a file this call never created. Linux reuses inodes eagerly enough for CI to catch it; that is why it surfaced in a hosted run rather than here.\n\nWhat makes this worse than an ordinary bug is what I wrote about it at the time. The d4f54392 artifact said the cleanup takes identity from the handle while open and compares the destination so that it removes nothing when identity cannot be confirmed. Every clause is true and the conclusion is false, because I treated inode equality as identity. The guard reads like a proof and is a heuristic.\n\nI will make the cleanup target only the file this call created, without depending on os.SameFile, and I will preserve the exclusive-creation contract the page and the tests rely on: of two concurrent creators exactly one wins and the other is refused. The shape I expect to reach for is creating at a unique sibling path and linking it into place, because link fails when the destination exists, which gives the same exclusivity while leaving cleanup a path nobody else can be holding. I will check that against the existing tests rather than assume it.\n\nTests will be deterministic and Linux-sensitive, reproducing the inode-reuse window rather than hoping for it, and I will show each one failing against the current code before the fix.\n\nWork goes on request/create-config-replacement-safety cut from current main, with an exact-head artifact, independent review, and the targeted, full, race, vet and diff gates your conditions name."}